WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.360
We're going to talk about a test called the sine test, which is the simplest useful hypothesis

00:15.360 --> 00:24.840
test that we'll encounter, so it's a good place for us to start. Now we'll consider the univariate

00:24.840 --> 00:37.920
case first, so we have data from some random variable, let's call it capital X. Now this

00:37.920 --> 00:43.960
test doesn't care what the distribution of X looks like, so let's suppose a density function

00:43.960 --> 00:49.680
of X, well it can be any old shape, so we'll have it looking like that, but it really doesn't

00:49.680 --> 01:02.880
matter. And the test concerns the location of the distribution as described by the median,

01:02.880 --> 01:12.680
which we denote theta. Now every distribution has a median, and it's such that we have half

01:12.680 --> 01:20.880
the distribution to the left of the median, theta, and half to the right.

01:20.880 --> 01:32.940
So if we had our data, and let's say we have an independent sample of size n from this random

01:32.940 --> 01:39.320
variable X, which has median theta, then we'd ask ourselves, well, you know, if the median

01:39.320 --> 01:49.320
really is theta, how would the data distribute themselves with respect to theta? Now, actually

01:49.320 --> 01:58.200
how they distribute themselves will be governed by this density function. But, for sure, if the

01:58.200 --> 02:09.300
median really is theta, we'd expect about half of our data to be greater than theta, then we'd

02:09.300 --> 02:23.300
about half to be less than theta. And that, simple idea, is the basis of our test. So we're

02:23.300 --> 02:31.300
testing the null hypothesis that the median of the random variable we're interested in, which, for simplicity

02:31.300 --> 02:53.300
here will denote by X, is some chosen value theta. Our alternative could be one-sided or two-sided. If the

02:53.300 --> 03:07.300
alternative is simply the negation of the null hypothesis, then that would be the two-sided case.

03:07.300 --> 03:17.300
And whether we're in a one-sided case or a two-sided case determines the set of values of the test statistic,

03:17.300 --> 03:31.300
which would lead to the rejection of the null hypothesis. So what is our test statistic in this case? Well, our test statistic

03:31.300 --> 03:59.300
we'll denote it by capital T, where T is the number of the observations, x1 up to xn, which are, let's say, greater than theta, the postulated value of the median, which would also be,

03:59.300 --> 04:21.300
which would also be, if we took theta away from each observation, so we look at x1 minus theta, and x2 minus theta, the number of these differences, that are positive.

04:21.300 --> 04:35.300
We could have equally have chosen less than, without really any loss. So as always in a hypothesis testing situation, we ask ourselves,

04:35.300 --> 04:49.300
how does this test statistic T behave under the null hypothesis? So if the median of the distribution from which the data are arising really is theta, how does T behave?

04:49.300 --> 05:07.300
Well, let's go back to our picture, and think to ourselves, well, if the data really are from a distribution with median theta, then each of these observations

05:07.300 --> 05:19.300
is just like tossing a coin, where we've got a probability of a half of being above theta, and a probability of a half of being below.

05:19.300 --> 05:33.300
So, we know the distribution that tells us the number of heads when we toss an unbiased coin.

05:33.300 --> 05:45.300
That's the binomial distribution, where n is the number of tosses, the number of observations in this case, and p is the probability of getting ahead,

05:45.300 --> 05:52.300
which in our case is the probability of an observation falling above the median, which is a half.

05:52.300 --> 05:59.300
So, under the null hypothesis.

05:59.300 --> 06:04.300
Of course, we know what this distribution looks like. So what does the binomial distribution look like?

06:04.300 --> 06:17.300
Well, we've got these values, let's denote them T, and we're distributed over the values 0, 1, up to n.

06:17.300 --> 06:28.300
And the picture one should have in one's mind is that this is a symmetrical distribution, and, well, if we toss a coin a lot of times,

06:28.300 --> 06:34.300
we don't expect to see 0 heads, at least not if the coins are biased, which is what we're assuming here.

06:34.300 --> 06:43.300
We don't expect to see hardly any heads, so these values down here have low probability, and these values up here would have low probability too.

06:47.300 --> 07:06.300
We may, if we choose, use the normal approximation to the binomial.

07:06.300 --> 07:14.300
Which would be useful to us if n is large, and we don't have access to a computer.

07:14.300 --> 07:28.300
Otherwise, if we have a computer, or if n is not too large, we can find the distribution of the test statistic under the null hypothesis exactly, without reference to an approximation.

07:28.300 --> 07:35.300
So let's have a little look at the example that's in the notes.

07:35.300 --> 07:59.300
The situation is about the weight gain of mice, we can assume these mice are independent of one another, in grams.

07:59.300 --> 08:06.300
On a new diet.

08:06.300 --> 08:15.300
So these mice apparently are on some new diet for a month, and we monitor their weight gains in grams.

08:15.300 --> 08:30.300
And the data are, well there's 15 observations, the first one was 20 grams, second observation 22, and so on.

08:30.300 --> 08:36.300
And the 15th is 24.

08:36.300 --> 08:51.300
And we're going to take our null hypothesis, that the median weight gain is 25 grams.

08:51.300 --> 09:06.300
And take the alternative hypothesis, let's take it that the median is less than 25 grams.

09:06.300 --> 09:17.300
So this is a one-sided, a one-sided case.

09:17.300 --> 09:23.300
So, to recap, we have mice on a new diet.

09:23.300 --> 09:36.300
We believe that the median weight gain on the diet may be 25 grams, or it may be, because maybe the 25 grams was some value from a previous diet.

09:36.300 --> 09:43.300
But it may be that we think that the new diet gives a lower weight gain, which is why we've chosen a one-sided case.

09:43.300 --> 09:54.300
In other words, we think it's improbable that the new diet would give a weight gain that's larger than 25 grams, so we're discounting that.

09:54.300 --> 10:06.300
And our alternative hypothesis is simply stating that the only alternative to the median being 25 grams is that it's actually less than 25.

10:06.300 --> 10:15.300
So what happens in our case is we're interested in the test statistic t, which counts up how many observations are greater than the median.

10:15.300 --> 10:18.300
The median as postulated in another hypothesis.

10:18.300 --> 10:23.300
So we'd go through our data, and we count up basically pluses and minuses.

10:23.300 --> 10:27.300
This is less than the postulated median, so that's a minus.

10:27.300 --> 10:31.300
That's a plus. We continue. That's a minus.

10:31.300 --> 10:42.300
And we count up the number of pluses, so that in this case, from the data in the notes, we observe that t is actually 4.

10:42.300 --> 10:48.300
So there were 4 observations greater than the hypothesized median of 25.

10:48.300 --> 11:05.300
Now under the null hypothesis, the number of observations greater than the median of 25, or the proposed median of 25, should follow the binomial distribution.

11:05.300 --> 11:14.300
In this case, 15 observations, each one equally likely to be greater than 25 or less than 25.

11:14.300 --> 11:24.300
So let's have a look at what this distribution looks like.

11:24.300 --> 11:34.300
So we've got probability over the set of values 0 up to 15, the integer value 0 up to 15.

11:34.300 --> 11:42.300
Of course, if we were to toss a coin 15 times, and it's an unbiased coin, very little probability of getting no heads, or one head,

11:42.300 --> 11:52.300
of course very little probability of getting 15 heads, or 14 heads, a little more getting 2, a little more getting 3, a little more getting 4, and so on.

11:52.300 --> 12:00.300
But the majority of the probability is on values closer to the mean of the distribution, which is 7.5.

12:00.300 --> 12:06.300
So the picture we have in our minds is something that's approximately like a normal curve.

12:06.300 --> 12:14.300
Now we've observed t equals 4.

12:14.300 --> 12:26.300
And we have to ask ourselves, well, which set of values would be more consistent with the alternative hypothesis than the null?

12:26.300 --> 12:38.300
Now if the median is actually less than 25 grams, which is what our alternative hypothesis says,

12:38.300 --> 12:44.300
then we would expect in our data to see a lot more negatives than positives.

12:44.300 --> 12:52.300
So we'd expect to see a lot more observations less than 25 grams than above, depending how much less the median is than 25, of course.

12:52.300 --> 13:00.300
So we would expect to see a low value of t, consistent more with the alternative than the null.

13:00.300 --> 13:10.300
Now when we're considering the p-value, we're therefore going to look at all of the observations, possible values of t,

13:10.300 --> 13:18.300
that were at least as inconsistent as that observed, as the one that we actually did observe.

13:18.300 --> 13:26.300
So we observed t equals 4, even more inconsistent would have been t equals 3, t equals 2, t equals 1, and so on.

13:26.300 --> 13:32.300
We can find from r, or doing the calculations on the computer or by tables,

13:32.300 --> 13:38.300
that that probability, the probability on these five possible values,

13:38.300 --> 13:43.300
each one at least as inconsistent with the null hypothesis as that we observed,

13:43.300 --> 13:53.300
is 0.059.

13:53.300 --> 13:55.300
So that's quite small.

13:55.300 --> 13:59.300
The question is, is it very small?

13:59.300 --> 14:08.300
Is it so small that we would reject the null hypothesis that the median weight gain is 25 grams?

14:08.300 --> 14:19.300
Well, there's certainly some evidence against the null hypothesis.

14:19.300 --> 14:27.300
Because this value that we observed of t is pretty small, if the median really is 25,

14:27.300 --> 14:33.300
because we'd expect to see values of t closer to the middle of the page.

14:33.300 --> 14:52.300
Nonetheless, it's not in the lower 5%, and at the 5% level, so at the 5% significance level,

14:52.300 --> 15:10.300
there is not enough evidence to reject the null hypothesis that the median weight gain is 25 grams.

15:10.300 --> 15:15.300
Probably, there's enough evidence to suggest that we should gather some more data.

15:15.300 --> 15:24.300
So that's a brief introduction to the sign test, and I hope that you've enjoyed it.

